WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.916 --> 00:00:12.368
Hello Kelly.

00:00:12.398 --> 00:00:15.099
First of all, a very big
thank you

00:00:15.129 --> 00:00:18.927
for accepting to do
this little interview.

00:00:19.356 --> 00:00:20.653
Thanks to you.

00:00:20.683 --> 00:00:24.971
Can you tell us
about yourself

00:00:25.583 --> 00:00:27.200
and what you're working on?

00:00:27.335 --> 00:00:28.527
All right, yes.

00:00:28.557 --> 00:00:30.330
Thank you very much, Liliane.

00:00:30.452 --> 00:00:33.601
I am Kelly Picard, I am French,

00:00:33.631 --> 00:00:36.285
a lecturer at the University Jean-Monnet,

00:00:36.315 --> 00:00:40.298
near Lyon, in public law.

00:00:40.328 --> 00:00:42.612
I work more specifically in public law.

00:00:43.298 --> 00:00:46.468
I've been working
for about ten years now

00:00:46.498 --> 00:00:50.855
on the notions of historical prejudice
and transitional justice.

00:00:50.885 --> 00:00:52.878
We will go back to this later.

00:00:53.036 --> 00:00:57.269
Specifically, I dedicated
my PhD thesis to these questions,

00:00:57.299 --> 00:01:00.619
namely, the question of
the responsibility of the State

00:01:01.028 --> 00:01:04.305
in the reparation
of historical damage.

00:01:05.453 --> 00:01:08.109
In addition,

00:01:08.139 --> 00:01:12.205
my research mainly deals

00:01:12.491 --> 00:01:16.036
with the protection
of rights and freedoms,

00:01:16.066 --> 00:01:18.881
mechanisms of justice

00:01:18.911 --> 00:01:21.939
and reparation
in post-conflict situations,

00:01:21.969 --> 00:01:25.214
typically, Truth
and Reconciliation Commissions,

00:01:25.244 --> 00:01:28.182
and the different forms of reparation
considered in such contexts,

00:01:28.212 --> 00:01:32.277
which somewhat fall
outside the typical legal framework.

00:01:33.112 --> 00:01:35.768
I also had the opportunity
to go into the field

00:01:35.798 --> 00:01:38.984
in order to assess the theories

00:01:39.014 --> 00:01:40.910
that I had developed during my research,

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:46.526
particularly for the Central African context
in Bangui, in the Central African Republic,

00:01:46.556 --> 00:01:52.300
where I was able to participate
in the process of reconstruction of the State,

00:01:52.330 --> 00:01:55.015
or at least in the post-conflict period
in recent years.

00:01:55.895 --> 00:01:56.983
There it is.

00:01:58.819 --> 00:02:00.186
Thank you.

00:02:01.905 --> 00:02:04.996
To get straight
to the core of this topic,

00:02:05.026 --> 00:02:08.549
you said that you are working
on historical prejudice.

00:02:09.143 --> 00:02:13.100
Can you tell us exactly what it is,
what you mean by that?

00:02:13.311 --> 00:02:16.095
Yes, of course.

00:02:16.125 --> 00:02:20.876
The first thing you need to know
about historical prejudice

00:02:20.906 --> 00:02:23.167
is that it doesn't actually
exist legally.

00:02:23.474 --> 00:02:27.164
So this is already a first obstacle.

00:02:27.194 --> 00:02:31.524
It doesn't exist, so it was
my concern to conceptualize it

00:02:31.554 --> 00:02:35.324
because even though legally,
it doesn't exist,

00:02:35.353 --> 00:02:39.201
the social impacts
that can be experienced

00:02:39.231 --> 00:02:42.100
through historical prejudice
are very present

00:02:42.396 --> 00:02:47.522
and therefore I found it necessary
to conceptualize the idea.

00:02:47.552 --> 00:02:50.155
So, what do I mean
by historical prejudice?

00:02:51.925 --> 00:02:53.740
Broadly speaking,

00:02:53.770 --> 00:02:58.449
it is the idea of a situation
of extreme violence,

00:02:58.790 --> 00:03:00.715
whether it is extreme
in its gravity

00:03:00.745 --> 00:03:02.995
or in its mass character.

00:03:03.025 --> 00:03:04.723
Extreme violence

00:03:04.982 --> 00:03:08.340
that has not found a response

00:03:08.370 --> 00:03:11.057
in terms of justice or reparation.

00:03:11.501 --> 00:03:14.212
Therefore, over time,

00:03:14.242 --> 00:03:17.901
the State has not provided
a satisfactory

00:03:18.601 --> 00:03:23.506
or sufficient response in terms of justice,
so justice in the broad sense,

00:03:23.536 --> 00:03:27.535
that includes precisely these questions
of reparation, truth, and so on.

00:03:28.329 --> 00:03:34.122
This has created a feeling
of resentment, frustration,

00:03:34.147 --> 00:03:38.103
anger, a certain form
of animosity, even mistrust,

00:03:38.417 --> 00:03:42.442
which is transmitted
from generation to generation

00:03:42.472 --> 00:03:44.420
and which,
as we have observed,

00:03:44.450 --> 00:03:48.175
is also reinforced
from generation to generation,

00:03:48.204 --> 00:03:54.014
until it potentially explodes
like a sleeping bomb.

00:03:54.043 --> 00:03:55.851
Françoise Sironi,
who is a psychologist,

00:03:55.881 --> 00:03:58.430
talks about the sleeping
bomb in society

00:03:58.461 --> 00:04:02.593
when we do not
take seriously

00:04:02.748 --> 00:04:06.097
these phenomena of violence
which are very serious

00:04:06.127 --> 00:04:09.317
and which we prefer to dismiss,

00:04:09.506 --> 00:04:12.572
to put under the carpet
in order to get rid of them,

00:04:12.602 --> 00:04:14.041
saying: "Let's move forward.

00:04:14.071 --> 00:04:15.493
The past is in the past, etc."

00:04:15.523 --> 00:04:17.173
These social phenomena...

00:04:17.582 --> 00:04:19.334
So, my concern

00:04:19.363 --> 00:04:23.808
was to try to give
a concrete and legal reality

00:04:23.838 --> 00:04:27.078
to this phenomenon and to try
to have it taken into account

00:04:28.405 --> 00:04:30.919
in court

00:04:30.949 --> 00:04:34.696
so that we can finally
bring a response

00:04:34.726 --> 00:04:38.692
in terms of reparation and justice
to the people who were

00:04:38.722 --> 00:04:41.430
in fact doubly victimized,
because they were victims

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:43.350
at the time of the violence,

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:45.821
victims of abuse,

00:04:45.851 --> 00:04:50.608
typically everything that is
a crime against humanity,

00:04:50.638 --> 00:04:54.314
genocide, war crimes,
therefore serious violence.

00:04:54.344 --> 00:04:56.782
Then, they are doubly
victimized

00:04:56.812 --> 00:05:00.969
by being denied
their status as victims

00:05:00.999 --> 00:05:02.806
and never recognized as such,

00:05:02.836 --> 00:05:05.338
and when the State never
recognizes their suffering

00:05:05.368 --> 00:05:07.676
and the prejudices
they have experienced and suffered.

00:05:08.011 --> 00:05:13.192
So, this reinforces once again
this feeling of injustice,

00:05:13.222 --> 00:05:18.039
a double injustice both in the State
that has allowed the violence

00:05:18.069 --> 00:05:19.907
to take place,

00:05:19.937 --> 00:05:25.337
and also in the State that has not
brought justice for this violence.

00:05:26.743 --> 00:05:30.161
This is what I mean
by historical prejudice.

00:05:32.767 --> 00:05:34.236
Thank you very much.

00:05:34.266 --> 00:05:37.017
Therefore, do the crimes
of colonization

00:05:37.047 --> 00:05:39.004
and the crimes of slavery

00:05:39.645 --> 00:05:42.104
fit into this category
or since

00:05:42.134 --> 00:05:44.456
the crimes of slavery
have been considered

00:05:44.486 --> 00:05:46.862
as crimes
against humanity,

00:05:46.892 --> 00:05:49.585
is it no longer necessary
to use this category?

00:05:54.064 --> 00:05:57.720
So, I include the crimes
of colonization and slavery

00:05:57.750 --> 00:06:00.084
in this category
of historical prejudice

00:06:00.114 --> 00:06:05.066
because there is indeed this double dimension
through historical prejudice.

00:06:05.096 --> 00:06:06.184
Why historical?

00:06:06.214 --> 00:06:08.524
Because there is this double
phenomenon in terms of history.

00:06:08.554 --> 00:06:12.852
There is history in the sense
that there has been a punctual event,

00:06:13.042 --> 00:06:16.326
a historical event
that has generated a prejudice.

00:06:16.356 --> 00:06:20.607
Then there are the historical facts
in time, diffuse, latent,

00:06:20.676 --> 00:06:23.534
which create
this historical dimension.

00:06:23.564 --> 00:06:26.980
In this double meaning,
namely old prejudices

00:06:27.010 --> 00:06:29.048
such as colonization

00:06:29.078 --> 00:06:31.808
or slavery, could fit
into this category.

00:06:32.574 --> 00:06:35.392
Then, if we enter

00:06:35.422 --> 00:06:38.057
into purely legal
and technical questions,

00:06:38.087 --> 00:06:40.064
we have the questions
of prescriptions,

00:06:40.094 --> 00:06:42.716
of the interest to act
which will come into question

00:06:42.746 --> 00:06:47.021
and will constitute obstacles
to legal actions.

00:06:47.169 --> 00:06:49.050
Perhaps we will
come back to this later,

00:06:49.080 --> 00:06:51.513
but typically

00:06:51.543 --> 00:06:56.472
both colonization and slavery
constitute historical prejudices

00:06:56.522 --> 00:07:00.666
to the extent that
they were characterized

00:07:00.696 --> 00:07:02.798
by extremely serious violence.

00:07:02.967 --> 00:07:05.977
In the French case,

00:07:06.007 --> 00:07:11.016
for example, colonial
slavery or the Algerian war

00:07:11.046 --> 00:07:16.181
did not receive a sufficient

00:07:16.211 --> 00:07:18.910
or satisfactory response
in terms of justice.

00:07:19.664 --> 00:07:22.749
So we would typically
approach these situations.

00:07:24.349 --> 00:07:26.916
To make sure we understand well,

00:07:26.946 --> 00:07:30.822
what do we actually gain by using
this category of historical prejudice

00:07:30.852 --> 00:07:33.266
rather than crimes
against humanity?

00:07:34.150 --> 00:07:39.119
Good question.

00:07:39.677 --> 00:07:41.999
The point is...

00:07:43.971 --> 00:07:45.171
Actually...

00:07:45.875 --> 00:07:49.082
So I'm just thinking about it,
so I don't say anything foolish.

00:07:50.063 --> 00:07:53.738
The idea of historical prejudice,
which is also underlying,

00:07:53.840 --> 00:07:59.731
is the idea of a prejudice
that is transgenerational.

00:07:59.758 --> 00:08:01.599
This is a little bit
the idea that I defend

00:08:01.624 --> 00:08:04.945
and which is a little bit
complicated for lawyers

00:08:04.970 --> 00:08:08.083
to conceive
in a very Cartesian mind.

00:08:09.313 --> 00:08:13.942
It is the idea that, in fact,
this violence, this phenomenon,

00:08:13.967 --> 00:08:18.422
the war crime
or crime against humanity,

00:08:18.447 --> 00:08:21.658
did not only have
repercussions on the people

00:08:21.683 --> 00:08:24.362
who were present
at the time of this violence,

00:08:24.387 --> 00:08:29.632
but it also had repercussions
on the descendants of these people,

00:08:29.657 --> 00:08:32.693
the descendants
of these direct victims.

00:08:32.717 --> 00:08:34.465
There were phenomena...

00:08:34.490 --> 00:08:38.161
When we study
psychology or sociology,

00:08:38.186 --> 00:08:40.798
or even now
neuroscience,

00:08:40.823 --> 00:08:43.486
there is more and more
research that tends to show

00:08:43.510 --> 00:08:46.795
that there is
a transmission of trauma,

00:08:46.820 --> 00:08:50.709
sometimes even
in the genetic heritage

00:08:50.734 --> 00:08:53.910
or in the psychological heritage.

00:08:53.935 --> 00:08:56.576
There really is a form
of transmission of traumas,

00:08:56.601 --> 00:09:00.263
injustices,
repercussions over time.

00:09:00.288 --> 00:09:02.099
Therefore, the idea also through...

00:09:02.124 --> 00:09:05.870
The idea of historical prejudices
would be to recognize

00:09:05.895 --> 00:09:10.611
also the potential effects
on all the successive generations

00:09:10.636 --> 00:09:13.396
and to understand better,

00:09:13.708 --> 00:09:16.782
the society as it is today

00:09:16.807 --> 00:09:19.721
and the social phenomena today.

00:09:19.746 --> 00:09:23.957
In fact, transgenerational prejudices

00:09:23.982 --> 00:09:29.032
tend to be taken into account
and considered a little more.

00:09:29.057 --> 00:09:34.123
Some lawyers
before the International Criminal Court

00:09:34.409 --> 00:09:37.169
are trying

00:09:38.384 --> 00:09:41.997
to focus on the question
of transgenerational harm,

00:09:42.022 --> 00:09:44.575
because it is specifically in the Katanga case -

00:09:44.600 --> 00:09:46.581
if I'm not wrong-

00:09:46.606 --> 00:09:49.768
concerning
the Democratic Republic of Congo.

00:09:50.590 --> 00:09:52.837
The Court agreed
to recognize...

00:09:52.862 --> 00:09:55.711
So it was a question
of crimes against humanity, war crimes.

00:09:55.736 --> 00:09:59.289
The Court agreed
to recognize transgenerational harm.

00:09:59.314 --> 00:10:04.152
So the fact that the descendants,
the children of people

00:10:04.177 --> 00:10:09.145
who experienced these wars, these attacks,
these events have suffered damage,

00:10:09.170 --> 00:10:14.526
psychological consequences,
sometimes physical consequences.

00:10:14.551 --> 00:10:18.539
We could elaborate,
but there is also a whole...

00:10:19.761 --> 00:10:21.180
The field of study

00:10:21.205 --> 00:10:24.702
that I also like
in these questions

00:10:24.727 --> 00:10:29.882
is the case of Rwanda, because in fact,
the effects of war rapes,

00:10:29.907 --> 00:10:34.131
at least on future generations,

00:10:35.210 --> 00:10:39.823
are particularly evocative
because women

00:10:39.848 --> 00:10:42.217
have contracted diseases,

00:10:42.242 --> 00:10:46.116
women who have given birth to children
as a result of these rapes.

00:10:46.141 --> 00:10:48.699
Thus, the effects
on the generations to come

00:10:48.724 --> 00:10:52.200
are particularly serious
and striking.

00:10:52.225 --> 00:10:53.789
We can observe them very well.

00:10:53.814 --> 00:10:57.823
The fact remains, and I return
to the International Criminal Court,

00:10:57.848 --> 00:10:59.677
it recognized these prejudices.

00:10:59.702 --> 00:11:02.033
It did not deny them,
it recognized them,

00:11:02.058 --> 00:11:04.039
but refused to repair them.

00:11:04.903 --> 00:11:09.207
It was considered
that the causal link

00:11:09.232 --> 00:11:12.750
couldn't be proved.

00:11:12.775 --> 00:11:18.154
It was difficult to really
prove that the pathologies

00:11:18.179 --> 00:11:21.262
that young children suffer today,

00:11:21.287 --> 00:11:23.364
the psychological disorders, and so on,

00:11:23.389 --> 00:11:27.395
were directly
and certainly attributable

00:11:27.420 --> 00:11:30.322
to the fact that their parents
had suffered this violence.

00:11:30.347 --> 00:11:33.302
So, it is the causal link that was somewhat lacking,

00:11:33.327 --> 00:11:37.220
but we are making progress,
step by step.

00:11:37.245 --> 00:11:40.430
So, just to answer your question
in one sentence,

00:11:40.455 --> 00:11:43.495
because I took a few
detours, the difference

00:11:43.520 --> 00:11:46.500
with crime against humanity
is that for crimes against humanity

00:11:46.525 --> 00:11:49.318
we only take into consideration
the direct victims,

00:11:49.343 --> 00:11:54.222
therefore, the first rank of victims
who are concerned by this violence.

00:11:54.247 --> 00:11:59.652
The idea would be
to see the global picture

00:11:59.677 --> 00:12:02.787
and all the repercussions
on society,

00:12:02.812 --> 00:12:06.452
because massive violence
such as war

00:12:06.477 --> 00:12:09.760
and crimes against humanity
do not only have repercussions

00:12:09.785 --> 00:12:13.052
on the people who
have experienced these phenomena.

00:12:13.077 --> 00:12:16.599
In my opinion, it has repercussions and disruptions,

00:12:16.624 --> 00:12:19.298
like a big ripple effect.

00:12:22.932 --> 00:12:24.653
Great, it's crystal clear.

00:12:24.678 --> 00:12:26.585
Thank you, perfect.

00:12:26.610 --> 00:12:30.111
And so, from there, do we...

00:12:30.136 --> 00:12:31.856
So, let's imagine

00:12:31.881 --> 00:12:35.438
that we manage
to go before a...

00:12:35.463 --> 00:12:38.175
Well, I don't know if it would be
before a national jurisdiction

00:12:38.200 --> 00:12:40.753
or an international jurisdiction,
would it be possible

00:12:40.778 --> 00:12:46.749
to have the responsibility
of a State like France recognized

00:12:46.774 --> 00:12:52.535
in the context of the crimes
committed in the former colonies?

00:12:53.566 --> 00:12:54.904
Yes.

00:12:54.929 --> 00:12:59.818
So, apparently a simple question,
but a very complex one.

00:12:59.843 --> 00:13:01.781
There are many things.

00:13:03.011 --> 00:13:05.334
There are many things because

00:13:05.359 --> 00:13:07.611
there is at the same time
the question of responsibility.

00:13:07.636 --> 00:13:09.736
We'll talk about the
responsibility of the State,

00:13:09.761 --> 00:13:11.371
but I'll come back to that.

00:13:11.396 --> 00:13:14.435
Then, there is also the question :
before which jurisdiction?

00:13:14.460 --> 00:13:17.404
Yes, indeed, there is
national or international.

00:13:18.625 --> 00:13:21.266
Just a word
on the responsibilities,

00:13:21.291 --> 00:13:23.435
it is true that here,
I focused

00:13:23.460 --> 00:13:25.534
on the idea of a responsibility
of the State,

00:13:25.559 --> 00:13:30.178
to seek a responsibility of a State
because one can also

00:13:30.203 --> 00:13:33.872
seek the responsibility
of individuals, individually,

00:13:33.897 --> 00:13:37.439
meaning private individuals,

00:13:37.464 --> 00:13:42.316
citizens or physical persons,

00:13:42.341 --> 00:13:45.128
but who are representatives
of the public authority,

00:13:45.153 --> 00:13:47.806
like a mayor
or a president of the Republic.

00:13:47.831 --> 00:13:50.326
It seems to me
that this does not fit

00:13:50.351 --> 00:13:51.938
with the idea
of historical prejudice

00:13:51.963 --> 00:13:55.262
because it is a succession
of responsibilities.

00:13:55.287 --> 00:13:58.884
There is not one person
responsible for all of this.

00:13:58.909 --> 00:14:01.874
It's a chain
of responsibilities.

00:14:01.899 --> 00:14:04.466
There is this idea
of historical continuity

00:14:04.491 --> 00:14:06.373
and everyone has
their share of responsibility.

00:14:06.398 --> 00:14:07.883
So, it bothers me...

00:14:07.908 --> 00:14:09.779
Well, it's not that
it bothered me.

00:14:09.804 --> 00:14:12.443
It's just that
these mechanisms

00:14:12.468 --> 00:14:15.775
that make individuals accountable
for their actions,

00:14:15.800 --> 00:14:16.876
already exist.

00:14:16.901 --> 00:14:19.313
So what I wanted
to explore a little bit is:

00:14:19.338 --> 00:14:22.123
can we hold a State accountable?

00:14:22.567 --> 00:14:24.551
This is already
a little more complicated

00:14:24.576 --> 00:14:27.371
because from
a penal point of view,

00:14:27.396 --> 00:14:28.745
it is not possible.

00:14:28.770 --> 00:14:32.691
It is not possible to engage
the criminal responsibility of a State,

00:14:32.716 --> 00:14:36.842
because in the criminal field,
there is this idea of moral element.

00:14:36.867 --> 00:14:39.074
In fact, there is the idea of intention.

00:14:39.099 --> 00:14:40.876
To hold, for example,

00:14:40.901 --> 00:14:46.221
accountable an individual
for a genocide,

00:14:46.246 --> 00:14:49.752
it is necessary to prove
before a judge

00:14:49.777 --> 00:14:54.122
that this individual had the intention
to exterminate a population.

00:14:54.932 --> 00:14:58.294
Now, in fact, this contradicts
the principle of a State,

00:14:58.319 --> 00:15:01.432
since it is not a person
who is thinking as such,

00:15:01.457 --> 00:15:06.128
so one cannot prove
the intention of a State legally,

00:15:06.153 --> 00:15:08.143
even tough one could question it.

00:15:08.168 --> 00:15:12.682
However, in the current context,
criminal responsibility and the State

00:15:12.707 --> 00:15:14.334
are not compatible.

00:15:14.359 --> 00:15:17.342
Therefore, these are public responsibilities.

00:15:19.992 --> 00:15:24.904
Thus, public means
the whole State community,

00:15:24.929 --> 00:15:26.988
all the State institutions.

00:15:27.013 --> 00:15:28.169
Yes?

00:15:28.194 --> 00:15:31.187
Just a quick question, can the criminal liability

00:15:31.212 --> 00:15:33.600
of a company be...

00:15:34.204 --> 00:15:37.396
The criminal liability
of a company?

00:15:37.421 --> 00:15:39.673
Good question.

00:15:42.719 --> 00:15:45.359
This is a bit out of
my field of expertise.

00:15:45.384 --> 00:15:46.497
It can be...

00:15:46.522 --> 00:15:50.257
I'm afraid I might be
getting ahead of myself.

00:15:50.282 --> 00:15:52.188
Yes, we can cut that part out.

00:15:52.213 --> 00:15:54.049
No, no, there's no problem.

00:15:54.074 --> 00:15:56.203
Maybe it's possible.

00:15:56.228 --> 00:15:58.500
Maybe it's possible,
but I have a little doubt.

00:15:58.676 --> 00:16:02.106
Then, there's always the difference
between private and public,

00:16:03.736 --> 00:16:07.403
and a company is private, so...

00:16:07.700 --> 00:16:10.545
In fact, as soon
as you are in public law,

00:16:10.575 --> 00:16:14.007
the State always
has derogatory rules.

00:16:14.037 --> 00:16:16.084
So, even though
there are things

00:16:16.114 --> 00:16:17.485
that are possible
in private law,

00:16:17.515 --> 00:16:19.509
they are not always possible
in public law.

00:16:19.684 --> 00:16:22.985
I admit that I prefer not
saying anything wrong.

00:16:23.015 --> 00:16:24.030
Okay.

00:16:24.060 --> 00:16:25.575
I don't want to make
a statement.

00:16:28.268 --> 00:16:33.115
To come back
to the public question,

00:16:33.145 --> 00:16:36.106
which is the collective
responsibility of the State.

00:16:36.862 --> 00:16:38.500
At this point, therefore,
it is possible,

00:16:38.530 --> 00:16:40.838
indeed, to activate
the responsibility of the State,

00:16:40.868 --> 00:16:45.938
both internally, nationally,
and internationally.

00:16:45.968 --> 00:16:47.688
Both cases or possible.

00:16:48.330 --> 00:16:51.458
On the internal level,
what we have, in France,

00:16:51.488 --> 00:16:56.160
are mechanisms of liability
for faults or without fault.

00:16:56.512 --> 00:16:58.667
Both are possible.

00:16:58.697 --> 00:17:01.977
So if it is for a fault, there is
an aggravating factor.

00:17:03.130 --> 00:17:05.592
This is what we call
administrative law.

00:17:06.336 --> 00:17:09.048
Then, there is
the international level

00:17:10.012 --> 00:17:12.740
where we have

00:17:12.770 --> 00:17:14.172
for example, go before

00:17:14.202 --> 00:17:18.515
the European Court
of Human Rights, before...

00:17:19.637 --> 00:17:24.089
So, eventually, the United Nations
Human Rights Committee,

00:17:24.117 --> 00:17:27.425
but it is not a jurisdiction
as such.

00:17:27.454 --> 00:17:29.637
The United Nations
Human Rights Committee

00:17:29.667 --> 00:17:31.356
makes recommendations,

00:17:31.386 --> 00:17:34.523
but it does not have
a binding action for sanctions.

00:17:34.553 --> 00:17:37.219
So, it's a little bit limited
in its effectiveness...

00:17:39.720 --> 00:17:41.858
However,
there are possibilities.

00:17:41.888 --> 00:17:44.718
It should be noted that earlier
I was talking about the ICC,

00:17:44.748 --> 00:17:46.256
the International Criminal Court.

00:17:46.286 --> 00:17:48.140
This is a court that is
only competent

00:17:48.170 --> 00:17:50.752
to address individual responsibilities.

00:17:50.782 --> 00:17:52.054
So, not States,

00:17:52.084 --> 00:17:54.916
but individuals
are prosecuted before the ICC.

00:18:00.789 --> 00:18:02.485
So, if we were

00:18:02.515 --> 00:18:07.476
to prosecute
the colonial crimes of France,

00:18:09.614 --> 00:18:12.204
what would be possible?

00:18:12.912 --> 00:18:15.980
I mean, I guess
it depends a little bit

00:18:19.075 --> 00:18:21.908
on where the crimes
were committed

00:18:21.938 --> 00:18:25.851
and whether we act
at the national,

00:18:25.881 --> 00:18:28.114
regional or international level?

00:18:28.711 --> 00:18:32.137
Yes, then indeed,

00:18:32.167 --> 00:18:36.787
we have several options
even if they are not all...

00:18:38.804 --> 00:18:43.961
I am an optimist
by nature, but I must admit

00:18:43.991 --> 00:18:47.374
that I am skeptical.

00:18:47.412 --> 00:18:48.960
In any case,
at the international level,

00:18:48.990 --> 00:18:52.197
if we start
at the international level,

00:18:52.227 --> 00:18:55.227
we don't have
many levers of action

00:18:55.257 --> 00:18:58.156
because
we may have

00:18:58.186 --> 00:18:59.604
the United Nations
Human Rights Committee,

00:18:59.634 --> 00:19:03.127
but as I said, it essentially
makes recommendations.

00:19:03.398 --> 00:19:08.133
So there are not necessarily
any binding means.

00:19:09.168 --> 00:19:14.685
Apart from that, we have
the European Court of Human Rights,

00:19:14.715 --> 00:19:19.727
but Algeria is not a member
of the European Council,

00:19:19.757 --> 00:19:22.738
so that would imply
a Franco-French action,

00:19:22.768 --> 00:19:26.995
even if it could be initiated
by activists, by associations

00:19:27.025 --> 00:19:31.130
or by descendants of victims,
it is quite possible.

00:19:32.432 --> 00:19:34.669
Moreover, there is at the moment,

00:19:34.699 --> 00:19:38.107
and the decision will be announced soon.

00:19:38.137 --> 00:19:42.426
An action before
the European Court of Human Rights

00:19:42.456 --> 00:19:46.369
concerning the reparation
of French colonial slavery.

00:19:46.807 --> 00:19:51.514
On the basis of this famous law,
the Taubira law of 2001,

00:19:51.544 --> 00:19:55.823
which recognizes colonial slavery
as a crime against humanity.

00:19:56.122 --> 00:19:58.034
It is actually a group,

00:19:58.064 --> 00:20:00.810
the Mouvement International pour les Réparations
(International Movement for Reparations),

00:20:00.840 --> 00:20:04.630
a collective, that has initiated
legal actions in France

00:20:04.660 --> 00:20:06.758
to demand reparations
because, in fact,

00:20:06.788 --> 00:20:09.173
they have underlined the fact

00:20:09.252 --> 00:20:13.453
that a penal qualification
has been established,

00:20:13.490 --> 00:20:15.862
that it has been recognized
that there was a crime against humanity,

00:20:15.892 --> 00:20:18.130
but that this has not been accompanied
by any reparations,

00:20:18.311 --> 00:20:22.346
which is peculiar because it has been
established that there is a crime,

00:20:22.376 --> 00:20:23.466
but no repairs have been made.

00:20:23.496 --> 00:20:25.301
Thus, they took
legal action

00:20:25.331 --> 00:20:26.961
before the French courts.

00:20:26.991 --> 00:20:30.840
Each time, they have been rejected
and so they have reached

00:20:30.870 --> 00:20:33.562
the European Court
of Human Rights,

00:20:33.592 --> 00:20:35.972
where they are trying
to defend their case.

00:20:36.002 --> 00:20:39.724
We will see what the Court will say,
even if, well, generally,

00:20:39.754 --> 00:20:41.493
it is not very daring

00:20:41.523 --> 00:20:43.808
on this kind of sensitive issue.

00:20:44.392 --> 00:20:48.986
However, it could be a way
to try to get some leverage,

00:20:49.016 --> 00:20:51.994
to put some diplomatic
pressure on.

00:20:52.024 --> 00:20:55.128
Therefore, we'll see
what happens.

00:20:56.564 --> 00:20:58.974
We need to point out
another issue.

00:20:59.004 --> 00:21:03.325
It is the temporal competence
of these courts.

00:21:03.940 --> 00:21:06.885
In fact, all these
international jurisdictions

00:21:07.311 --> 00:21:09.843
are quite recent

00:21:10.908 --> 00:21:15.119
and, for example,
the European Court of Human Rights,

00:21:15.149 --> 00:21:18.867
France only ratified the statute,
so it only accepted

00:21:18.897 --> 00:21:21.896
the jurisdiction
of the Court in 1974.

00:21:22.312 --> 00:21:26.163
This means that everything
that happened before 1974,

00:21:26.360 --> 00:21:28.083
cannot be considered by the Court.

00:21:28.655 --> 00:21:32.943
If we take the case of the ICC,
the International Criminal Court,

00:21:32.973 --> 00:21:34.805
came into force in 2002.

00:21:34.835 --> 00:21:38.156
The same thing applies
to all the facts prior to 2002,

00:21:38.186 --> 00:21:39.641
which the Court
cannot determine.

00:21:40.284 --> 00:21:42.207
Therefore, we have
a real problem

00:21:42.237 --> 00:21:45.135
with the question
of the Algerian war.

00:21:45.165 --> 00:21:47.507
It happened a long time ago,

00:21:47.537 --> 00:21:50.677
and thus, the competence
of the courts is limited.

00:21:51.087 --> 00:21:55.293
Hence my bias, I actually
try to go through

00:21:55.323 --> 00:21:58.805
the back door
of transgenerational prejudices

00:21:58.835 --> 00:22:03.306
and to recognize the fact that,
even if the facts are old,

00:22:03.391 --> 00:22:05.506
the repercussions
on individuals

00:22:05.536 --> 00:22:06.819
are still present

00:22:06.849 --> 00:22:11.311
and these actions
still have consequences.

00:22:11.341 --> 00:22:14.585
Therefore, there are still prejudices
today that are suffered,

00:22:14.775 --> 00:22:19.701
but it is a little difficult
to get the courts to recognize it.

00:22:20.488 --> 00:22:24.951
There is this barrier,
that is temporal,

00:22:24.981 --> 00:22:25.951
which means that
there is no way around it.

00:22:25.981 --> 00:22:27.552
There is no retroactive application.

00:22:27.582 --> 00:22:30.454
A court cannot take up
facts that took place

00:22:30.484 --> 00:22:32.984
before it was created.

00:22:33.014 --> 00:22:34.399
That is a real problem.

00:22:36.348 --> 00:22:39.805
On the other hand,
there is the possibility of creating

00:22:39.835 --> 00:22:42.415
institutions that are
somewhat specialized,

00:22:42.445 --> 00:22:44.388
like we did, for example,

00:22:44.418 --> 00:22:47.496
for the International Criminal Tribunal
for the former Yugoslavia

00:22:47.526 --> 00:22:52.001
or for Rwanda,
creating specific jurisdictions

00:22:52.270 --> 00:22:56.329
that will deal with past events,

00:22:57.197 --> 00:23:01.398
but they will be created for this purpose,
but this also requires

00:23:01.880 --> 00:23:04.216
a certain international consensus

00:23:04.246 --> 00:23:06.580
and a political will to create them.

00:23:07.135 --> 00:23:11.191
So that's at the international level.

00:23:11.221 --> 00:23:13.439
At the national level,

00:23:13.469 --> 00:23:17.584
there is still the possibility
of referring to the domestic courts.

00:23:18.835 --> 00:23:23.949
We are also left with a somewhat
Franco-French question.

00:23:23.979 --> 00:23:25.090
Finally, there is not this...

00:23:25.120 --> 00:23:27.864
Although there is not
this idea

00:23:27.894 --> 00:23:32.147
that would be important
of cooperation

00:23:32.554 --> 00:23:34.444
between Algeria and France.

00:23:34.474 --> 00:23:35.713
So, we remain...

00:23:35.743 --> 00:23:37.373
Well, these are
the current debates.

00:23:37.403 --> 00:23:40.606
We approach the subject
a little from our French perspective.

00:23:40.636 --> 00:23:42.737
However, it can be a way

00:23:42.767 --> 00:23:45.685
to move things forward
in terms of reparation.

00:23:46.096 --> 00:23:48.657
The difficulty is to find

00:23:48.687 --> 00:23:52.357
the right legal basis,

00:23:52.605 --> 00:23:56.366
to justify the prejudice
and the causal link.

00:23:56.649 --> 00:24:01.366
In fact, for liability,
three things must be demonstrated.

00:24:01.396 --> 00:24:03.502
You have to show
a causal event.

00:24:03.527 --> 00:24:05.522
You have to show a prejudice

00:24:06.537 --> 00:24:09.403
and a causal link
between the event

00:24:09.428 --> 00:24:12.376
and the prejudice
and often the difficult part

00:24:12.401 --> 00:24:17.548
it is to show the 100 %
causality between the two.

00:24:19.689 --> 00:24:23.555
That's where
the courts rush in

00:24:23.580 --> 00:24:28.956
to dismiss this type of litigation

00:24:30.024 --> 00:24:33.104
and once again,
sweep it under the carpet.

00:24:34.858 --> 00:24:37.643
So, if I understand you
correctly,

00:24:37.668 --> 00:24:41.810
the alternative would be
to try to mobilize

00:24:41.835 --> 00:24:45.687
as much as possible this category
of historical prejudice.

00:24:46.132 --> 00:24:50.866
That would be the alternative?

00:24:50.891 --> 00:24:54.577
Well, yes,
but I'm preaching

00:24:54.602 --> 00:24:56.899
to my choir here,
but of course

00:24:56.924 --> 00:25:00.558
that's how I approached it.

00:25:00.583 --> 00:25:03.244
After that, there is
everything that is...

00:25:03.269 --> 00:25:07.080
In fact, when I worked
on these questions for my thesis,

00:25:07.105 --> 00:25:09.892
I was quickly confronted...

00:25:09.917 --> 00:25:11.909
I considered
all the possible mechanisms

00:25:11.934 --> 00:25:13.427
of responsibility.

00:25:13.452 --> 00:25:15.280
I saw that even if each time,

00:25:15.305 --> 00:25:17.674
there is a small step missing
to be able to get there,

00:25:17.699 --> 00:25:21.907
these are obstacles
and as I said,

00:25:21.932 --> 00:25:26.422
well the lawyers, they are straightforward,
Cartesian, so all these things

00:25:26.447 --> 00:25:29.114
and the fact that
there are repercussions

00:25:29.139 --> 00:25:31.610
on the bodies and the minds
of the generations after,

00:25:31.635 --> 00:25:33.551
it is something that is
going to take a little bit

00:25:33.576 --> 00:25:36.456
of time to process.

00:25:36.481 --> 00:25:38.857
So, there is another possibility,

00:25:38.882 --> 00:25:43.069
which is not to resort
to the legal route at all,

00:25:43.752 --> 00:25:47.660
that is to say that we must
ask ourselves the question :

00:25:47.685 --> 00:25:48.739
what do we want?

00:25:48.764 --> 00:25:52.328
If what we want is
a form of reparation,

00:25:52.353 --> 00:25:55.314
but that maybe translates

00:25:55.339 --> 00:25:57.553
into official public recognition,

00:25:57.578 --> 00:26:01.272
into public policies,

00:26:01.297 --> 00:26:04.274
whatever they may be,

00:26:04.299 --> 00:26:06.581
whether it is a bit
of positive discrimination,

00:26:06.606 --> 00:26:09.497
whether it is access
to care,

00:26:09.997 --> 00:26:12.492
whether it is compensation.

00:26:12.517 --> 00:26:15.682
In reality, you don't necessarily need
to use the legal system,

00:26:15.707 --> 00:26:18.040
and that is why I looked into

00:26:18.065 --> 00:26:20.492
transitional justice,
because it actually is

00:26:20.517 --> 00:26:24.771
the essence of transitional justice.

00:26:24.796 --> 00:26:30.696
It is a justice that faced
with the obstacles of its environment

00:26:30.721 --> 00:26:34.546
and the rigidity of the law,
will work around it,

00:26:34.571 --> 00:26:38.103
use various ways and set up

00:26:38.128 --> 00:26:42.030
paralegal systems
that borrow a little bit

00:26:42.055 --> 00:26:47.067
from the ceremonial of justice,
but that fit next to

00:26:47.092 --> 00:26:50.355
and in parallel
with institutional justice.

00:26:50.380 --> 00:26:53.683
Hence, typically,
the Truth and Reconciliation Commissions,

00:26:53.708 --> 00:26:57.073
like those made
in South Africa or elsewhere.

00:26:57.098 --> 00:27:01.924
There is also an option
that is quite interesting to explore,

00:27:01.949 --> 00:27:04.683
and which is starting
to come up quite a bit,

00:27:04.708 --> 00:27:07.076
which is what we call
courts of opinion.

00:27:07.101 --> 00:27:11.591
Tribunals of opinion
have been set up, for example,

00:27:11.616 --> 00:27:13.899
in the field of environmental law.

00:27:13.924 --> 00:27:17.311
The Monsanto tribunal is
actually a court of opinion.

00:27:17.336 --> 00:27:20.216
So, given that
environmental law was not

00:27:20.241 --> 00:27:22.631
sufficiently developed,
we still do not have a consensus

00:27:22.656 --> 00:27:25.543
on the crime of ecocide,
and so on,

00:27:25.568 --> 00:27:28.722
well, a simulation
of the tribunal is made.

00:27:28.747 --> 00:27:33.241
We recreate all the ceremonial
of a court with experts,

00:27:33.266 --> 00:27:36.985
public figures, and we pretend

00:27:37.010 --> 00:27:39.258
to judge the facts in question.

00:27:39.283 --> 00:27:43.548
This was also done
for what concerns the comfort women

00:27:43.573 --> 00:27:47.979
during the Second World War,

00:27:48.004 --> 00:27:50.845
these Korean women
who were used

00:27:50.870 --> 00:27:55.414
by Japan to satisfy
the needs of Japanese soldiers.

00:27:55.439 --> 00:27:59.694
Faced with the reluctance
of the justice system to recognize the fate

00:27:59.719 --> 00:28:01.285
of these women,

00:28:01.310 --> 00:28:03.220
they set up
courts of opinion.

00:28:03.245 --> 00:28:08.001
So, in fact, they acted
as if they had judged the perpetrators,

00:28:08.026 --> 00:28:11.754
whether they were individuals or states,
the Emperor in this case,

00:28:11.779 --> 00:28:15.905
according to rules of justice,

00:28:15.930 --> 00:28:18.501
either borrowing
from existing law

00:28:18.526 --> 00:28:21.766
or inventing them
to show how the law

00:28:21.791 --> 00:28:24.458
should evolve, and in fact,
this has this effect, afterward,

00:28:24.483 --> 00:28:29.589
of diplomatic, political and symbolic
pressure that is also very strong.

00:28:29.614 --> 00:28:35.318
So, there are other avenues
that can go beyond

00:28:35.343 --> 00:28:40.341
what exists and the jurisdictional
and institutional side,

00:28:40.366 --> 00:28:45.241
that is sometimes very rigid
and not flexible enough

00:28:45.266 --> 00:28:47.257
to understand these situations

00:28:47.282 --> 00:28:50.723
that are a bit
outside the legal category.

00:28:53.595 --> 00:28:55.310
Last question, quickly:

00:28:55.335 --> 00:29:00.820
did these courts of opinion

00:29:00.845 --> 00:29:03.786
make it possible
to change the lines?

00:29:04.872 --> 00:29:08.219
Yes, but courts

00:29:08.244 --> 00:29:12.991
have very slowly
moved the lines

00:29:13.016 --> 00:29:16.138
regarding the comfort
women, for example.

00:29:16.163 --> 00:29:19.776
They have progressively...

00:29:19.801 --> 00:29:24.319
well, it's been decades,

00:29:24.344 --> 00:29:28.446
but they are beginning
to get some kind of recognition

00:29:28.840 --> 00:29:33.509
from Japanese institutions,
but it's still very limited.

00:29:33.534 --> 00:29:36.064
But anyhow, there is also
an international awareness

00:29:36.089 --> 00:29:37.954
on these questions.

00:29:37.979 --> 00:29:39.947
This is also the goal,
to shed light

00:29:39.972 --> 00:29:42.191
on these issues so that
others can take them up

00:29:42.216 --> 00:29:43.632
and the movement can grow.

00:29:43.657 --> 00:29:46.861
As for Monsanto, for example,
in environmental law,

00:29:46.886 --> 00:29:49.602
where there have been
more concrete repercussions

00:29:49.627 --> 00:29:53.390
and because there are also
environmental issues,

00:29:53.415 --> 00:29:55.918
it is a little more consensual
and it is a little easier

00:29:55.943 --> 00:29:58.835
perhaps to bring them,
but there are a whole lot of...

00:29:58.860 --> 00:30:02.094
There are a lot of court
of opinion experiences

00:30:02.119 --> 00:30:05.611
of which I don't know
well enough the repercussions

00:30:05.636 --> 00:30:07.206
afterward to know if

00:30:07.231 --> 00:30:09.862
it has been systematically
followed up.

00:30:09.887 --> 00:30:11.832
But it's also...

00:30:11.857 --> 00:30:16.598
This remains in fact the limit of the law,
even institutional law,

00:30:16.623 --> 00:30:20.733
it is subject to political will
and therefore, even if

00:30:20.758 --> 00:30:24.513
we have a classic legal decision
or a legal decision

00:30:24.538 --> 00:30:28.396
that is a little
more simulated

00:30:28.421 --> 00:30:31.685
according to processes
that are a little more innovative,

00:30:31.710 --> 00:30:34.523
well, if the political power
does not want to follow,

00:30:34.548 --> 00:30:38.373
does not want
to enforce these decisions,

00:30:39.713 --> 00:30:44.923
it remains difficult to impose it,
as they remain in control of its own realm.

00:30:44.948 --> 00:30:50.125
So, it remains quite difficult
to move the lines

00:30:50.150 --> 00:30:53.294
and time is

00:30:53.319 --> 00:30:57.603
a powerful ally
in these mechanisms,

00:30:57.921 --> 00:31:00.659
but to make
one last small parallel,

00:31:00.684 --> 00:31:02.955
with regard
to the Second World War,

00:31:02.980 --> 00:31:06.529
at the beginning, individual responsibilities,

00:31:06.554 --> 00:31:09.403
therefore, of the perpetrators,
were recognized.

00:31:09.428 --> 00:31:11.645
For example,
with the Nuremberg tribunals,

00:31:11.670 --> 00:31:14.006
perpetrators were condemned.

00:31:14.031 --> 00:31:17.920
However, to recognize the collective
and global responsibility of the State,

00:31:17.945 --> 00:31:20.561
we had to wait,
in the case of France,

00:31:20.586 --> 00:31:25.292
until 2009, for a first
court decision in France

00:31:25.317 --> 00:31:28.561
to recognize
the responsibility of France

00:31:28.586 --> 00:31:31.138
in the deportation
of the Jews of France.

00:31:31.163 --> 00:31:36.913
So, 60 years,
when it came to the Holocaust,

00:31:36.938 --> 00:31:39.965
and so time, really,
is an important element,

00:31:39.990 --> 00:31:42.076
even if it never goes fast enough.

00:31:42.101 --> 00:31:45.081
I can understand that,
but when you look at it,

00:31:45.106 --> 00:31:48.476
it's almost the same timeframe
with the Algerian war,

00:31:48.501 --> 00:31:51.169
it almost ended 60 years ago.

00:31:51.194 --> 00:31:53.965
So, we are perhaps
in this pivotal moment.

00:31:53.990 --> 00:31:56.104
That's why we feel
that things are starting to move.

00:31:56.129 --> 00:31:57.929
Maybe it's those 60 years.

00:31:57.954 --> 00:32:00.565
There is perhaps
a generational element here,

00:32:00.590 --> 00:32:03.140
which means that
it begins to emerge

00:32:03.165 --> 00:32:06.787
and that we can perhaps hope
that it will move a little more.

00:32:10.541 --> 00:32:13.000
Very good, that's great.

00:32:13.627 --> 00:32:17.880
Listen, I'm looking forward
to the discussion that we can have

00:32:17.905 --> 00:32:23.397
with both activists and students
on these subjects.

00:32:23.422 --> 00:32:26.303
Yes, I hope it can shed
a little bit of light

00:32:26.328 --> 00:32:28.000
and give some food for thought.

00:32:28.025 --> 00:32:29.031
Yes.

00:32:29.056 --> 00:32:30.717
Thank you very much.

00:32:30.742 --> 00:32:32.987
Thank you Liliane!

